\details{
In R, a board option is its ID and the value that a script gives it, which
\code{new_board_option()} makes. The constructors of core's board options, such
as \code{new_n_rows_option(25L)}, make one each and compute nothing, neither a
default nor a check. A board option made without a value has none. The board
then holds no value for it, so that whatever needs it takes the default of
its definition in the client.

Core ships three board options: \code{n_rows}, the rows of a table output's page,
\code{show_conditions}, the severities of a block's conditions that a frontend
shows, and \code{dark_mode}, which switches between the light and the dark theme.
Core's basic frontend requires \code{show_conditions} and \code{dark_mode}.
So the client gives every board that lacks a value of either one its
default as the board opens.
Their schemas ship beside core's runtime, and R writes the value that a
script gives by them (see \code{\link[=blockr_ser]{blockr_ser()}}).

Each of core's board options takes its default from the blockr option of
its name, which the client asks R for the first time that something on the
page requires the board option (see \code{\link[=blockr_option]{blockr_option()}}).
So \code{options(blockr.n_rows = 25L)}, or the environment variable
\code{BLOCKR_N_ROWS}, still sets the rows of a page on a board that holds no
value for \code{n_rows}.
Without a blockr option, \code{n_rows} takes 10, \code{show_conditions} warnings and
errors, and \code{dark_mode} the theme that the user's system prefers.

Several board options make a \code{board_options} object, which
\code{new_board_options()} makes, holding one board option per ID. The predicate
\code{is_board_options()} tests for one, \code{as_board_options()} coerces to one and
\code{validate_board_options()} checks one. The values that it holds, by ID, are
given by \code{board_option_values()}, and \code{combine_board_options()} combines
sets of board options, keeping the first for each ID.
